# /etc/default/sloth-rf — site configuration for sloth-rf.service (#215).
#
# Read by systemd as an EnvironmentFile: KEY=value, one per line, no
# shell expansion. `make install-service` installs this file only when
# there is none, so an edited copy is never replaced. A host whose
# decoders are still read by the Wi-Fi sensor gets its flags moved here
# by `sloth-migrate-rf --apply`, from /etc/default/sloth
# (docs/wiki/deployment.md §11.4).
#
# The unit fixes two paths (--db /var/lib/sloth-rf/sloth-rf.db and
# --data-socket unix:/run/sloth-rf/sloth-rf.sock); the sandbox admits no
# others, so they do not go here.
#
# Extra arguments appended to ExecStart, split on whitespace. These are
# the inputs: with none of them sloth-rf has nothing to read.
#   --adsb PATH           a local readsb's aircraft.json (wiki: adsb), e.g.
#                         --adsb /run/readsb/aircraft.json. The unit
#                         admits /run/readsb read-only; the sloth user
#                         still needs the file's mode or group to allow
#                         the read.
#   --adsb sbs://127.0.0.1:30003
#   --adsb beast://127.0.0.1:30005
#                         the decoder's SBS or Beast output instead
#                         (loopback only, read-only). These need AF_INET:
#                         the gpsd block of
#                         /usr/local/share/sloth/sloth-rf-gps.conf.
#   --aprs PATH           the packet log a local Dire Wolf writes
#                         (direwolf -L FILE; wiki: aprs), e.g.
#                         --aprs /var/lib/direwolf/aprs.log
#   --aprs-freq HZ        the frequency that decoder is configured for,
#                         e.g. --aprs-freq 144390000
#   --aprs-silent-hours N report the decoder silent after N hours with
#                         no packet (default 6)
#   --rtl433 PATH         the JSON event log a local rtl_433 writes
#                         (rtl_433 -F json:FILE; wiki: rtl433), e.g.
#                         --rtl433 /var/lib/rtl_433/events.jsonl
#   --no-sdr-trim         start every decoder input whatever USB lists.
#                         By default sloth-rf looks at /sys/bus/usb once
#                         at startup and, with no SDR there, does not
#                         start a file --adsb, --aprs (with --aprs-freq
#                         and --aprs-silent-hours) or --rtl433: one
#                         journal line names each, and slothweb shows it
#                         "no SDR attached" instead of down. So one file
#                         serves a host with SDRs and one without.
#                         --adsb sbs:// / beast://, --gps, --location and
#                         --victron are never trimmed, nor is a decoder
#                         file written in the last 10 min (--adsb) or
#                         24 h (--aprs, --rtl433): something else feeds
#                         it (a sound-card TNC, readsb net-only,
#                         rtl_tcp). With nothing left
#                         to read it runs on idle. An SDR
#                         plugged in later needs
#                         `systemctl restart sloth-rf`. Give this flag
#                         only where an SDR is attached but its USB ID
#                         is not one sloth knows (wiki: cli-reference).
#   --victron PATH        the JSONL sloth-va writes (wiki: victron), e.g.
#                         --victron /var/lib/sloth-va/victron.jsonl
#   --gps gpsd://127.0.0.1:2947
#                         the GNSS receiver, through gpsd (needs the same
#                         AF_INET drop-in). On a host that also runs the
#                         Wi-Fi sensor with --gps, both must use gpsd: a
#                         serial receiver opens once.
#   --gps /dev/ttyACM0    a serial NMEA receiver read directly, only
#                         where sloth-rf is its one reader (the serial
#                         block of the same drop-in)
#   --gps-baud N          serial speed for --gps /dev/... (default 9600)
#   --gps-allow-remote    permit a gpsd that is not on loopback
#   --location LAT,LON[,ALT]
#                         a fixed position instead of, or behind, --gps:
#                         WGS84 decimal degrees and metres
#   --jsonl FILE          also write the records to a file. Off by
#                         default; /var/lib/sloth-rf is the only place
#                         the sandbox lets the daemon write.
#   --strict              the fail-closed profile (wiki: cli-reference)
#   --site-label LABEL    name stamped on what this daemon reports
# Example:
#   SLOTH_RF_ARGS=--adsb /run/readsb/aircraft.json --gps gpsd://127.0.0.1:2947 --site-label attic-sensor
SLOTH_RF_ARGS=
